(11) Tj T*
(Every agent, however simple or sophisticated, is built from five parts. The November 2025) Tj T*
(Introduction to Agents whitepaper covers each in depth.² For our purposes here, the) Tj T*
(short version:) Tj T*
(* The model is the reasoning engine. It reads the current context, decides what should) Tj T*
(happen next, and produces the next thought, the next tool call, or the next message.) Tj T*
(* Tools connect the model to the world. They include APIs the agent can call, code it can) Tj T*
(execute, databases it can query, and other agents it can delegate to.) Tj T*
(* Memory is the state. It allows the agent to recall past interactions, retrieve project-) Tj T*
(specific rules, and retain context across sessions so it never starts from a blank slate.) Tj T*
(* Orchestration is the code that runs the loop. It assembles the context for each model) Tj T*
(call, dispatches tool calls, captures their results, and decides whether to continue.) Tj T*
(* Deployment is what turns the prototype into a service: hosting, identity, observability, and) Tj T*
(the production infrastructure the agent runs on.) Tj T*
(These four parts work together in a continuous loop: get the mission, scan the scene, think) Tj T*
(it through, take action, observe and iterate. The loop is the beating heart of every agent.) Tj T*
(Everything else in this paper, and everything in the rest of the course, is a variation on) Tj T*
(this loop.) Tj T*
(What is vibe coding?) Tj T*
(In February 2025, Andrej Karpathy posted a description of a new way of programming that) Tj T*
(resonated widely across the software engineering community. He described an approach) Tj T*
(where you "fully give in to the vibes, embrace exponentials, and forget that the code even) Tj T*
ET
endstream
