(May 2026) Tj T*
(12) Tj T*
(exists." In this mode, a developer describes what they want in natural language, accepts the) Tj T*
(AI's output, and when something breaks, copies the error message back into the prompt and) Tj T*
(asks the AI to fix it.2) Tj T*
(The term went viral because it captured something real: many developers were already) Tj T*
(working this way but hadn't had language for it. Within months, "vibe coding" became a) Tj T*
(common descriptor for any AI-assisted development workflow, which created confusion. Is a) Tj T*
(senior engineer using an AI assistant to implement a well-specified feature "vibe coding"? Is) Tj T*
(a team using AI agents to execute a carefully planned architecture? The term was applied so) Tj T*
(broadly it began to lose meaning.) Tj T*
(By early 2026, Karpathy himself acknowledged that the original framing was too narrow,) Tj T*
(introducing the term "agentic engineering" to describe the more disciplined end of) Tj T*
(the spectrum.4) Tj T*
(The spectrum: vibe coding to agentic engineering) Tj T*
(Rather than treating vibe coding and agentic engineering as a binary, we find it more useful) Tj T*
(to think of them as endpoints on a spectrum. The key differentiator is not whether you use) Tj T*
(AI. It's how much structure, verification, and human judgment surrounds the AI's output.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
