(21) Tj T*
(How AI transforms each phase) Tj T*
(Requirements and planning) Tj T*
(Requirements is the phase where the gap between intent and implementation has historically) Tj T*
(been widest. Translating business needs into technical specifications has been a manual,) Tj T*
(error-prone process that creates a persistent gap between what stakeholders want and what) Tj T*
(engineers build.) Tj T*
(Modern AI tools can participate directly in requirements refinement: generating user stories) Tj T*
(from product briefs, identifying edge cases that humans miss, producing API schemas from) Tj T*
(natural-language descriptions, and generating interactive prototypes from specification) Tj T*
(documents. Agentic development environments allow developers to go from a description to) Tj T*
(a working prototype in minutes, collapsing the requirements-to-prototype feedback loop to) Tj T*
(near zero.) Tj T*
(Requirements stop being a document handed off between teams. They become) Tj T*
(a conversation between humans and AI that produces specification and initial) Tj T*
(implementation simultaneously.) Tj T*
(Design and architecture) Tj T*
(Architecture remains the most stubbornly human-centric phase of the SDLC, and for) Tj T*
(good reason. Architectural decisions are fundamentally about trade-offs: consistency vs.) Tj T*
(availability, complexity vs. flexibility, build vs. buy. These trade-offs depend on business) Tj T*
(context, organisational constraints, and long-term strategic considerations that AI cannot) Tj T*
(fully grasp.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
