(May 2026) Tj T*
(23) Tj T*
(AI also transforms test generation itself. Agents can produce test cases, including edge) Tj T*
(cases and property-based tests, that humans might not think of. More importantly, tests and) Tj T*
(evals become the primary mechanism for communicating intent to AI agents: a well-written) Tj T*
(eval suite tells the AI what "correct" means and provides an automated way to verify it.) Tj T*
(These practices are most effective when wired into a continuous quality flywheel: evaluate) Tj T*
(against a benchmark suite, diagnose failures by clustering root causes, optimize the prompts) Tj T*
(or tools that caused them, verify fixes against a regression suite, and monitor production) Tj T*
(traffic for new failure modes. Each cycle compounds.) Tj T*
(Code review and deployment) Tj T*
(The review process itself is being augmented, with AI serving as a first-pass reviewer that) Tj T*
(can identify potential bugs, style violations, security vulnerabilities, and performance issues) Tj T*
(before a human reviewer sees the code. This does not replace human review, since context-) Tj T*
(dependent decisions about design, maintainability, and strategic alignment still require) Tj T*
(human judgment, but it significantly reduces the cognitive burden on reviewers.) Tj T*
(Deployment pipelines are becoming AI-aware as well. AI agents can monitor deployment) Tj T*
(health, automatically roll back problematic releases, and predict deployment risks based) Tj T*
(on the nature and scope of changes. Modern deployment platforms increasingly integrate) Tj T*
(with AI-powered observability to create feedback loops between production behaviour and) Tj T*
(development decisions.) Tj T*
(Day 5 in this series covers what changes for human reviewers when PR volume scales with) Tj T*
(agent output -- bundled summaries, conditional LGTM, agent-driven code-review skills.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
