(Where to start) Tj T*
(The shift from syntax to intent is not a future state. It is the work in front of us today. Whether) Tj T*
(reading this as an individual builder or as a leader thinking about how a team or organisation) Tj T*
(adopts these tools, the same underlying principle holds: AI amplifies the engineering culture) Tj T*
(it lands in. The practices below translate that principle into action.) Tj T*
(For individual developers) Tj T*
(1. Set up an AGENTS.md \(or equivalent\) for the project. Pick the convention that matches) Tj T*
(the coding agent of choice. Start with ten lines: stack, conventions, hard rules, workflow.) Tj T*
(Add a rule every time the agent does something it should not do again.) Tj T*
(2. Install a set of skills for your coding agents \(like Agents CLI\) to build, evaluate, deploy) Tj T*
(and optimize agents.) Tj T*
(3. Pick one repetitive workflow and make it the first agent. A research workflow, a code) Tj T*
(review process, a recurring report, a piece of content produced regularly. Use a coding) Tj T*
(agent for the prototype, and graduate it to a production agent through Agents CLI when it) Tj T*
(earns its keep. Building one agent end to end teaches more than reading about a hundred.) Tj T*
(4. Write the tests and evals before generating the code. Together they are the contract) Tj T*
(with the AI. A well-written test and eval suite communicates intent more precisely than) Tj T*
(any natural-language prompt, and turns AI-assisted development from vibe coding into) Tj T*
(agentic engineering.) Tj T*
(5. Review every line the agent produces that is going to ship. Be skeptical of anything) Tj T*
(that looks clever. Check imports for real packages. Verify that error handling covers) Tj T*
(realistic failure modes. Code that the team does not understand becomes debugging cost) Tj T*
(the team cannot afford.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
